Since the 1950s, numerous research studies, including experiments, theoretical analyses, and numerical simulations, have been conducted to investigate thermal shock-induced fracture behaviors and to determine the TSR performance of ceramic materials. In theoretical research, the critical thermal stress fracture theory based on thermo-elasticity and the thermal shock damage theory based on fracture mechanics proposed by Kingery [4] and Hasselman [5], respectively, have been widely used. Although new theories have been established in recent years, most of them focused on predicting the critical thermal shock temperature difference [9-13]. A few researchers focused on the thermal shock-induced fracture analysis. For example, Qi et al. [14] introduced a fractal geometric method to characterize the crack patterns of ceramic bars after thermal shock. Li et al. [15] theoretically analyzed the interfacial thermal shock-induced fracture behavior for the thermal barrier coating system with a segmented structure. Thus far, none of the published theories are capable of revealing thermal shock-induced crack initiation and propagation. Even though the theoretical analysis utilized fracture mechanics, it still failed to characterize the cracking process by using the simple assumption of periodically distributed straight cracks and one-dimensional (1D) thermal transfer. In experimental studies, multi-crack propagation with periodic and hierarchical characteristics in brittle thin strips under severe thermal shock caused by water quenching was observed by Bahr et al. [16]. These main characteristics of thermal shock-induced cracking were also found by Jiang et al. [17] and Liu et al. [18] by using thin rectangle and circular slab specimens of Al2O3 ceramic via both water quenching experiments and numerical methods. Li et al. [19] controlled the water entry posture of Al2O3 cuboid bar specimens during the quenching test and found that the thermal shock-induced fracture was strongly affected by the water entry posture of specimens. Shao et al. [20,21] observed the thermal shock-induced multiple cracking process and determined the crack propagation speed of ceramic thin strips by high-speed imaging. However, the method is limited to two-dimensional (2D) conditions and fails to uncover the main controlling mechanisms of the thermal shock-induced multiple cracking behavior and periodic parallel cracks phenomenon. Currently, most of the thermal shock studies for three-dimensional (3D) conditions only demonstrate the crack patterns and residual strength of specimens [14,19,22], but they fail to explain the thermal shock-induced crack evolution processes from the crack initiation to propagation until the final failure. Numerical methods are considered as a feasible solution to study the mechanisms and controlling factors of complex thermal shock-induced cracking behaviors compared to both theoretical and experimental methods. For example, the non-local failure model [23,24] and damage mechanics-based model [25] in the frame of the finite element method (FEM), and the peridynamic (PD) model [6] have been applied to investigate the thermal shock-induced fracture of ceramics, including the effects of the specimen size, heterogeneity of materials, and initial thermal shock temperature. However, these widely used numerical methods still exhibit limitations in simulating complicated thermal shock-induced multi-crack propagation, and most of them focus on modeling 2D cases. Specific criteria based on fracture mechanics for crack evolution are required for the numerical methods based on the FEM, which are valid for 3D conditions. It is not easy to link the fracture criterion of the PD method to the strength of materials, and the method does not consider the effect of temperature. Therefore, the mechanisms of thermal shock-induced fracture are hard to be investigated by the PD method. The phase-field method is capable of modeling crack propagation by introducing a global continuous auxiliary scalar named the phase-field variable to label the states of the material from intact to damaged. The method transforms the crack evolution from a surface problem with discontinuities to a volume problem by the smooth continuum governing equations that can be solved directly by the FEM or other numerical methods. Moreover, the prediction of the initiation location of cracks, the path of propagation, and the crack deflection and branching are conveniently realized by the phase-field method but fail to be done by using the traditional Griffith fracture theory [26,27]. With the convenience of the phase-field method, the quasi-static crack propagation [26-31] and dynamic cracking behaviors [32-37] of brittle materials have been studied. In [38-43], the phase-field method was applied to investigate the crack propagation for nonlinear fracture problems. In the framework of the FEM, the phase-field method is highly capable of modeling multi-physics coupling fractures, such as thermo-mechanical fracture, chemical–mechanical fracture, and hydraulic fracture by virtue of its continuum character. The initiation and selective propagation of thermal shock- and drying-induced cracks for brittle materials were studied by Bourdin et al. [44], and the simulation results agreed well with the experimental observations. Miehe et al. [45] developed a fully coupled thermo-mechanical PFM for brittle fracture, and the thermal shock-induced cracking process of a glass strip and solid brick was investigated. Chu et al. [7], Wang et al. [46], Mandal et al. [47], and others [48,49] have studied the thermal shock-induced fracture of ceramic materials by the PFM and the simulation results demonstrated some characteristics of experiment results. Although the above-mentioned PFM simulations of thermal shock-induced fracture were consistent with the experimental results of thin ceramic slabs under water quenching, the modeling results suggested damages at unexpected places where there was no crack in experimental results. In addition, the phase-field modeling neglected the effects of temperature on material properties. In this paper, a phase-field model for thermo-mechanical coupling fracture is developed to study the thermal shock-induced fracture of ceramics. Aiming at eliminating unexpected damage initiation, a temperature-dependent fracture energy threshold is introduced in the model. The effects of temperature-dependent material properties and the interaction between crack propagation and heat transfer were taken into consideration. The rest of the paper is organized as follows. The phase-field model for thermo-mechanical coupling fracture with a temperature-dependent fracture energy threshold is introduced in Section 2. Subsequently, numerical implementation of the model is performed by using an Abaqus/Standard UEL subroutine in Section 3. In Section 4, thermal shockinduced cracking behaviors of the thin slabs and circular specimens of Al2O3 ceramic under water quenching are investigated as 2D cases by the proposed model. Complex crack propagation behaviors of ceramic bars under water quenching-induced thermal shocks in 3D conditions are simulated, in which the water entry posture of ceramic bars during the quenching process is taken into consideration. The simulation results demonstrating the essential features are consistent with the experimental results. Several conclusions are drawn out in the last section.
