4. Results and discussions
4.1. Water quenching thermal shock of thin Al2O3 slabs
The water quenching test is the most widely used method to evaluate the TSR of ceramics. In the experimental study [17], the ceramic slabs were first heated to different initial thermal shock temperatures and then dropped freely into a water bath. The geometric dimensioning of the specimen is shown in Fig. 2. Because of the symmetry, only half of the ceramic slab was modeled with the symmetric displacement boundary condition. All the material properties are shown in Table 1[17-19]. The Young’s modulus, Poisson’s ratio, density, and fracture strength of Al2O3 are almost unchanged in the range of 0–600 ◦C. However, the thermal expansion, thermal conductivity, and specific heat of Al2O3 are strongly dependent on temperature. The initial temperatures T0 of the slabs were set to be
300, 400, and 500 ◦C, and the temperature of the water bath was held constant at 20 ◦C. The heat transfer coefficient between the slab
surface and water was set to be 55 kW⋅m 2⋅K 1 according to [17]. Because the inconel wires were positioned 3–4 mm from the ends of the specimens in the experiment [17], when the simulation results were compared with experimental results, only the measured region (see in Fig. 3) was taken into consideration. As shown in Fig. 4, the thermal shock-induced crack topographies of the PFM simulations are faithfully consistent with the experimental results under elevated initial thermal shock temperatures. A higher initial thermal shock temperature results in more cracks and smaller space among the long cracks. The cracks could be classified into two levels according to the crack length. The long cracks have lengths greater than 30% of the height of the specimen. Those with lengths smaller than this value are considered as short cracks. The regularity of the crack lengths along the horizontal direction is consistent with the experimental results. The process of crack initiation and propagation during thermal shock in the measured region under the initial temperature of 500 ◦C is shown in Fig. 5. All cracks including short ones initiate to the total number first, which is the so-called first thermal shock regime [7]. Then the selected cracks propagate to a certain length in the second thermal shock regime until the final patterns are generated. This cracking behavior was demonstrated by Shao et al. [20] and captured by our simulation here. Moreover, the PFM simulation uncovers the controlling mechanism that the tensile elastic energy dominates the crack initiation and propagation, which cannot be obtained in the experiment. Fig. 4(a)–(c) shows that the lengths of the long cracks simulated by the proposed PFM under different initial thermal shock temperatures match well with the experimental results, and most of the long crack tips reach the yellow datum lines. The present model performs better than the model proposed in [46] in Fig. 4 (d) because their model obtained long cracks with shorter lengths than those revealed by the experiments. To quantitatively evaluate the numerical results, the number of long cracks is further compared in Fig. 6. Cracks with lengths greater than 30% of the slab height are defined as long cracks. The number of the long cracks increases with the increase of the initial thermal shock temperature in both simulation and experimental results. Moreover, the simulation results of thermal shock by the phase-field method reported in [7,44] and Fig. 4(d) showed unexpected phase-field damage initiated at positions where there were no cracks observed in experiments. The phase-field damage even evolved on the whole slab surface. As shown in the governing equations and Eq. (15), the phase-field damage would reduce the material stiffness ruled by degradation function g(d). However, the material remained intact with initial stiffness in the places where there were no cracks in experiments. On the contrary, the simulation results shown in Fig. 4(a)–(c) and Fig. 5 with temperature-dependent fracture energy threshold completely avoid this issue. No unexpected phase-field damage is observed at the place without cracks and the material remains intact. These characteristics confirm the capability of the proposed phase-field model to reproduce the thermal shock-induced cracking behaviors of ceramics in the framework of the FEM and show the importance of the temperaturedependent fracture energy threshold in the phase-field modeling of thermal shock-induced cracking.
