5. Conclusions
A 3D thermo-mechanical phase-field modeling method for thermal shock-induced fracture in ceramics was proposed in this paper. By introducing a temperature-dependent fracture energy threshold in the model, the unexpected damage evolution was eliminated. With the use of the FEM, the phase-field method was first validated by investigating the thermal shock-induced behaviors of Al2O3 ceramic materials, including the thin-slab and thin-circle specimens which represented ortho-symmetric and axial symmetric conditions respectively. Results indicated that the simulated crack patterns were consistent with the experimental results. The initiation and propagation of thermal shock-induced cracks, multi-cracks propagation, and hierarchy of long and short cracks were well reproduced in PFM simulations. No damage was initiated at unexpected places where there were no cracks found in the experiment. Afterwards, the 3D simulation of the thermal shock-induced behaviors of cuboid bar Al2O3 specimens under water quenching was carried out. The net-like topologies of thermal shock-induced cracks at the front and back surfaces were captured and the effect of the water-entry direction of cuboid-bar specimens on the thermal shock-induced cracking behaviors was also faithfully consistent with the experimental results. The bottom surface only formed the transverse cracks, and the top surface which was the last part submerged in water showed the longitudinal cracks along the z-direction crossing over the transverse cracks along the x-direction. Moreover, both the 2D and 3D simulation results revealed the mechanism that the tensile part of the strain energy controlled the initiation and propagation of the thermal shock-induced cracks in ceramics. The proposed model was capable of overcoming the limitations of other models based on the widely used criteria, such as the maximum principal stress and maximum tensile stress.
